    "post_rendered": "<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > is an odd positive integer and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/4/b6400c6fe3f1ed70e5afd387a9686cdc3fa09531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" > are two rational numbers satisfying <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/0/aa0588e33dbf8155a4bf25ee82a8fc54437f54eb.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"$$x^n+2y=y^n+2x.$$\"  width=\"148\" height=\"16\" >Prove that <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/0110f1bb04c51b2a4d431bf14489fc1b48b37421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" >.</span>",
    "post_canonical": "$n$ is an odd positive integer and $x,y$ are two rational numbers satisfying $$x^n+2y=y^n+2x.$$Prove that $x=y$.",
