   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 3160599,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 546186,
    "poster_id": 153333,
    "post_rendered": "Call admissible a set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"13\" > of integers that has the following property:<br>\nIf <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/1/e31a6d551914e2a240995edf06a23743622cfa7c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y \\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" > (possibly <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/1/0110f1bb04c51b2a4d431bf14489fc1b48b37421.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"43\" height=\"11\" >)</span> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/0/770e6f38556b43e23ada135f45c381be86fcfcaa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+kxy+y^2 \\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"145\" height=\"18\" > for every integer <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\"  width=\"9\" height=\"12\" >.</span><br>\nDetermine all pairs <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/6/b/46ba6ed097cc88ded349bf99eea89817f135f470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"11\" > of nonzero integers such that the only admissible set containing both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\"  width=\"15\" height=\"8\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > is the set of all integers.<br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Warut Suksompong, Thailand</i>",
    "post_canonical": "Call admissible a set $A$ of integers that has the following property:\nIf $x,y \\in A$ (possibly $x=y$) then $x^2+kxy+y^2 \\in A$ for every integer $k$.\nDetermine all pairs $m,n$ of nonzero integers such that the only admissible set containing both $m$ and $n$ is the set of all integers.\n\n[i]Proposed by Warut Suksompong, Thailand[/i]",
    "username": "lyukhson",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
