   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 3160560,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 546169,
    "poster_id": 153333,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/8/578cf6c5b8058f6f8e6369416da6dcaf73f8fc01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"43\" height=\"14\" > be an integer. What is the maximum number of disjoint pairs of elements of the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/e/5eede1c4a2afb425a0b9c04e45e60d072472c4c3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\{ 1,2,\\ldots , n \\}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"95\" height=\"18\" > such that the sums of the different pairs are different integers not exceeding <span style=\"white-space:nowrap;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >?</span>",
    "post_canonical": "Let $n \\geq 1$ be an integer. What is the maximum number of disjoint pairs of elements of the set $\\{ 1,2,\\ldots , n \\}$ such that the sums of the different pairs are different integers not exceeding $n$?",
